Done. Round summary: the decisive fix was found in the disassembly — gcc was compiling every `set1_pd(ur[j])` broadcast in the unrolled L=13 kernels into a port-5 `vpermpd`, saturating the one port the FMAs and shuffles both need. A targeted `"+m"` asm barrier forces them through memory as `{1to8}` FMA operands (load-port work), which fixed the one cell I was losing (13 B=512 m=1: 21.5 → 30.4 GF/s, now ahead of fftw_measure) and lifted the B=1 and chained cells too. On top of that: an always-inlined two-transform pair kernel sharing table loads for batched L=13, and single-accumulator-set batched variants for both sizes. All correctness gates pass on every size/batch/chain shape with 2–5 decades of margin, output bit-repeatable; strategy record appended with the failed experiments (`"+v"` pinning, single-set at B=1) and their killing numbers.

One line, as requested: dense symmetric-pair DFT with memory-barrier `{1to8}` broadcasts + inlined pair kernel — wallaby best 0.0155 µs/transform at B=1 and 0.0079 µs batched (L=13; L=31: 0.0327 / 0.0315), rel L2 = 1.8e-16 (L=13) / 2.9e-16 (L=31).
